{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Stereo Window Placement.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Stereo Window Placement.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"stereo window placement stereo window placement","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Stereo Window Placement.md","fileName":"Stereo Window Placement.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Stereo Window Placement","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 18:17\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Stereoscopic Imaging and Conversion]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Stereoscopic Imaging and Conversion","Stereo Parallax and Convergence","digitalvisualeffectsandcompositing.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Stereo Window Placement.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Stereo Window Placement.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"stereo window placement stereo window placement","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Stereo Window Placement.md","fileName":"Stereo Window Placement.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Stereo Window Placement","heading":"Stereo Window Placement","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Aligning the two eye images at a particular subject establishes the depth that seems to coincide with the physical screen, or stereo window. Aligning a distant subject makes nearer material appear in front of the screen; aligning a nearer subject makes more of the scene appear behind it. The book compares several alignments of three depth-staged models to show the difference. Dramatic foreground pop-out is a creative option, not a default; excessive separation can strain the viewing experience. See [[Stereo Parallax and Convergence]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Stereoscopic Imaging and Conversion","Stereo Parallax and Convergence","digitalvisualeffectsandcompositing.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embedding